{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/10/codegen_prompts/3.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/10`\n- 题号：`3`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/10/3.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"3\">\n- `description`：用隔板将网格分成多个房间，每个房间内的\"种子\"像素沿隔板上的缺口反方向生长出与缺口等宽的矩形条，直到碰到对面墙壁。\n- `Input structure`：背景为 black。网格被若干条完整的 maroon 水平线和竖直线切割成矩形房间（类似棋盘格）。每条 maroon 分隔线上恰好有一个由背景色构成的缺口（连续的 1-3 像素宽的中断）。部分房间内含有一个单像素的彩色种子（颜色各异，如 red、blue、green 等），种子不接触任何 maroon 线。\n- `Rule`：第一步：对每个含种子的房间，检查该房间四面墙壁（maroon 线段）上哪一面存在缺口。第二步：种子从自身位置出发，沿缺口所在墙壁的反方向（即背离缺口、朝向对面墙壁）生长出一个矩形色条。色条的宽度等于缺口的像素宽度，色条的长度从种子延伸到对面墙壁（不覆盖墙壁本身）。色条以种子所在列（或行）为中心对齐。第三步：种子本身保留，maroon 线和缺口保持不变，其余背景不变。\n- `Key insight`：缺口不是出口而是方向指示器——生长方向是缺口的反方向；缺口宽度编码了色条宽度。解题者需要同时发现方向反转规则和宽度传递规则。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/84551f4c, train/e3fe1151, train/fc10701f, train/f18ec8cc, eval/89565ca0, train/6ca952ad, train/5adee1b2, train/37ce87bb\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3472\nmechanism=0.4462\nlexical=0.0192\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:marker, operation:count, output:same_size, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/84551f4c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/84551f4c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars on the background; blue bars start chains and red bars may follow\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each blue-started chain, rotate each reached vertical bar clockwise onto the shared baseline; the next red bar is reached when its column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3127\nmechanism=0.3851\nlexical=0.0143\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:axis, object:marker, object:rectangle, operation:count, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e3fe1151.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3fe1151.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"four equal corner regions divided by a solid background cross, each with one background-colored blank pixel\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each blank with the color needed so every corner region has the same color multiset, using the maximum observed count of each color across the regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc10701f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3089\nmechanism=0.4124\nlexical=0.0223\ncode_feature=0.2000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:axis, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, operation:count, output:same_size, relation:aligned, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/fc10701f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/fc10701f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"solid orange rectangle s\",\n  \"path\": \"straight horizontal or vertical corridor from s to the aligned same-sized maroon rectangle d\",\n  \"dst\": \"same-sized maroon rectangle d aligned with s on one axis\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint red only at corridor openings flanked by black segments perpendicular to the path\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase old s to background; replace d with orange; keep black segments and all other cells unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f18ec8cc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2960\nmechanism=0.3607\nlexical=0.0137\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:hole, object:marker, operation:count, output:same_size, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/f18ec8cc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/f18ec8cc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nReorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\nSmall marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\nRule\nIf adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\nfrom right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\nthe far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\nKey insight\nThe transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\ntheir source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"vertical color-major blocks s, including their internal marker pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"left-to-right output block slots d with the same widths as the ordered source blocks\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"rank(s) == rank(d) after applying the block-order rule\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy each whole source block unchanged into its assigned output slot\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"default order is a one-block left shift; if adjacent shared-color marker rows read the current block colors right-to-left, use reverse order\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2992\nmechanism=0.3764\nlexical=0.0160\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:axis, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, operation:count, relation:aligned, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/89565ca0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"colored partitioned frame line drawings; ignore scattered pad-color pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack one horizontal bar per frame, ordered by count of enclosed background regions then color id\",\n  \"transform\": \"bar length = number of enclosed 4-connected background regions; pad right with the scattered color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height = number of frame colors, width = maximum enclosed-region count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2900\nmechanism=0.3519\nlexical=0.0136\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:hole, object:marker, operation:count, output:same_size, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6ca952ad.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6ca952ad.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
